/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An engine control loop has one thrust-setting variable and several limits.
   - An engine control acts on three things: fuel flow (the fuel metering valve), air flow (variable stator vanes and compressor bleed valves, which keep the compressor away from surge) and, on some engines, the exhaust (nozzle area on afterburn
   - A FADEC (Full Authority Digital Engine Control) is an electronic engine control with two channels, one active and one standby.
   - The lever asks for a rating, not a fuel flow.
   - N1c is the corrected fan speed in % (what the rating fixes); N1 is the physical fan speed in %; θ is the inlet temperature ratio; T1 is the inlet total temperature in kelvin; 288.15 K is the sea-level standard temperature.
   - On a hot day the same corrected speed needs a higher physical N1 and runs a higher EGT.
   - Answer.
*/
