/* book Worked example: Q: A FADEC sets the take-off rating as a corrected fan speed N1c = 97.0 %. The physical N1 limit is 100.0 %. Find the physical N1 the FADEC commands at an inlet temperature of (a) 15 °C, (b) −10 °C and (c) 40 °C. (d) At 40 °C, what corrected speed can the engine actually reach?
   1. Use N1 = N1c × √θ with θ = T1 ÷ 288.15 K, temperatures in kelvin.
   2. (a) 15 °C = 288.15 K: θ = 1.0000, √θ = 1.0000, so N1 = 97.0 %.
   3. (b) −10 °C = 263.15 K: θ = 263.15 ÷ 288.15 = 0.9132, √θ = 0.9556, so N1 = 97.0 × 0.9556 = 92.7 %.
   4. (c) 40 °C = 313.15 K: θ = 313.15 ÷ 288.15 = 1.0868, √θ = 1.0425, so the schedule asks for 97.0 × 1.0425 = 101.1 %. That is above the 100.0 % limit, so the FADEC holds N1 at 100.0 %.
   5. (d) At the limit, N1c = 100.0 ÷ 1.0425 = 95.9 %, below the 97.0 % rating, so take-off thrust is lower on the hot day.
   Answer:  */
